WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.949
Picture a detective, called to an office building at ten in the morning.

2
00:00:06.949 --> 00:00:09.074
Tenants are waiting with complaints.

3
00:00:09.074 --> 00:00:11.080
A wet ceiling on the fourth floor.

4
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.910
Flickering lights on the third.

5
00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:14.680
A smell of damp on the second.

6
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.123
A tidy detective writes one report per complaint, and goes home.

7
00:00:19.123 --> 00:00:20.641
It feels like progress.

8
00:00:20.641 --> 00:00:24.072
Each complaint is real, and each one gets an answer.

9
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:30.792
A good detective walks upstairs, and finds one cracked pipe on the fifth floor, which explains all of them.

10
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.171
What nobody asked was how the complaints connect.

11
00:00:34.171 --> 00:00:35.724
Hold on to that picture.

12
00:00:35.724 --> 00:00:38.572
Today's tool tries to be the good detective.

13
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.703
Now a made-up shop.

14
00:00:40.703 --> 00:00:47.287
Web calls checkout, checkout calls payments, and payments keeps its data in a Postgres database.

15
00:00:47.287 --> 00:00:50.236
At 10:02, four alerts fire within a minute.

16
00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:51.675
Every alert is true.

17
00:00:51.675 --> 00:00:54.634
And not one of them says which caused the others.

18
00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:57.655
Which one is the cause, and which are just echoes?

19
00:00:57.867 --> 00:01:01.050
With the stack we built ourselves, we go digging.

20
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.363
A dashboard shows three services went bad together.

21
00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:07.677
A trace shows a long database span inside payments.

22
00:01:07.677 --> 00:01:09.691
A log search for that trace ID.

23
00:01:09.691 --> 00:01:13.004
We get there, in three tools, matching IDs by hand.

24
00:01:13.233 --> 00:01:17.209
You might be thinking: easy, the cause is whatever went bad first.

25
00:01:17.209 --> 00:01:20.282
Let's be fair, that's where most of us would start.

26
00:01:20.282 --> 00:01:22.752
But all four went bad in the same minute.

27
00:01:22.752 --> 00:01:25.463
Timing alone can't tell a cause from an echo.

28
00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:28.617
What we need is the plumbing plan: who calls whom.

29
00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:32.073
Dynatrace draws that plan by itself.

30
00:01:32.073 --> 00:01:37.900
Its agent, OneAgent, runs on every machine and records each call as it passes through.

31
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.152
Dynatrace calls each thing it watches an entity.

32
00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:45.039
The calls become links on a live map, called Smartscape.

33
00:01:45.039 --> 00:01:49.498
Web calls checkout, checkout calls payments, payments uses Postgres.

34
00:01:49.498 --> 00:01:52.843
The building's plumbing plan, redrawn every minute.

35
00:01:53.067 --> 00:02:00.434
An engine called Davis watches every entity against its own baseline, what normal looks like for it at this time.

36
00:02:00.434 --> 00:02:04.280
At 10:02, web, checkout and payments leave their baselines.

37
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.403
Davis starts at the affected entity and walks down the links.

38
00:02:08.403 --> 00:02:10.003
Where will the trail end?

39
00:02:10.003 --> 00:02:11.027
Pause and guess.

40
00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:15.394
Start at web.

41
00:02:15.394 --> 00:02:16.287
Unhealthy.

42
00:02:16.287 --> 00:02:18.342
What does it depend on?

43
00:02:18.342 --> 00:02:19.145
Checkout.

44
00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:21.913
Is checkout unhealthy too?

45
00:02:21.913 --> 00:02:22.305
Yes.

46
00:02:22.305 --> 00:02:23.872
Keep going down.

47
00:02:23.872 --> 00:02:24.753
Payments?

48
00:02:24.753 --> 00:02:26.223
Also unhealthy.

49
00:02:26.223 --> 00:02:27.300
Keep going.

50
00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:28.233
Postgres?

51
00:02:28.233 --> 00:02:30.875
The obvious guess is the database.

52
00:02:30.875 --> 00:02:34.449
The slow spans were database calls, after all.

53
00:02:34.449 --> 00:02:35.304
Let's look.

54
00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:37.026
Postgres looks healthy.

55
00:02:37.026 --> 00:02:43.843
The waiting happens inside payments, in its connection pool, the fixed set of open connections it shares.

56
00:02:43.843 --> 00:02:45.985
Every call queues for a free one.

57
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.807
So the trail ends at payments: an unhealthy component whose own dependencies look fine.

58
00:02:51.807 --> 00:02:54.192
That is the most probable root cause.

59
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.584
The database was the alibi everybody believed.

60
00:02:57.584 --> 00:02:59.661
The pipe cracked one floor up.

61
00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:03.721
Dynatrace calls this walk a fault-tree analysis.

62
00:03:03.721 --> 00:03:15.348
Instead of four alerts, it opens one problem: an ID like P-12345, the category SLOWDOWN, payments as the root cause, and web and checkout listed as affected.

63
00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:17.799
Four complaints, one cracked pipe.

64
00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:22.855
And notice how Davis linked them: by following recorded calls, not by timing.

65
00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:25.572
Now let's break it on purpose.

66
00:03:25.572 --> 00:03:28.380
Picture payments running without OneAgent.

67
00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:29.917
Nobody instrumented it.

68
00:03:29.917 --> 00:03:31.789
Where does the walk end now?

69
00:03:31.789 --> 00:03:32.859
Pause and guess.

70
00:03:36.067 --> 00:03:36.877
At checkout.

71
00:03:36.877 --> 00:03:40.727
Davis can't follow a call into something nobody measures.

72
00:03:40.727 --> 00:03:43.767
So it names checkout, which is only a victim.

73
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.436
So the point really is: Davis can only follow what something measured.

74
00:03:48.436 --> 00:03:51.287
An unwatched service is where its trail ends.

75
00:03:51.287 --> 00:03:52.681
A pipe with no sensor.

76
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:54.863
A few honest limits.

77
00:03:54.863 --> 00:03:57.894
Davis doesn't know what you promised your users.

78
00:03:57.894 --> 00:04:03.956
It compares each service with its own past, so a service that has always been slow looks normal.

79
00:04:03.956 --> 00:04:07.429
Your service level objectives are still yours to write.

80
00:04:07.633 --> 00:04:16.301
The one-agent, one-map approach is paid for in money, in lock-in, and in control: your data lives on Dynatrace's servers.

81
00:04:16.533 --> 00:04:19.528
And OneAgent only sees a program from its start.

82
00:04:19.528 --> 00:04:25.955
A process that was already running when you installed it shows up with no traces, until you restart it.

83
00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:28.428
So the moral of the story.

84
00:04:28.428 --> 00:04:30.217
Don't answer each complaint.

85
00:04:30.217 --> 00:04:40.568
Follow the pipes to the one that cracked: start at the symptom, walk down what it depends on, and stop at the first broken thing whose own dependencies look fine.

86
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:41.758
A quick check.

87
00:04:41.758 --> 00:04:44.427
Davis names checkout as the root cause.

88
00:04:44.427 --> 00:04:48.738
But you know checkout calls a service that nobody instrumented.

89
00:04:48.738 --> 00:04:50.106
What do you suspect?

90
00:04:50.106 --> 00:04:51.201
Pause and think.

91
00:04:54.433 --> 00:04:56.844
That the trail ended at the blind spot.

92
00:04:56.844 --> 00:05:01.047
The real cause may sit one step further down, where nobody measured.

93
00:05:01.047 --> 00:05:02.901
Instrument it, or go and look.

94
00:05:03.133 --> 00:05:08.173
Next, we get our own data into Dynatrace, and learn the language that reads it.

95
00:05:08.173 --> 00:05:12.256
How do you ask one question of metrics, logs and traces at once?
